Hi everyone,
I am new to SECR analysis and I am building models for leopards in certain protected areas in Southern India. I will eventually move on to other animals we have camera-trapped (wolves and jackals) in other regions. I was thinking of building an exhaustive list of models involving variations in lambda0 and sigma (using at least b, bk, t and T along with a covariate sex). However I saw Murray mentioning  <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/emaoCZOnmXw/m/TYy-JbUyBgAJ>that building such an exhaustive list of models checking is not a good practice. Then what would you recommend for choosing appropriate models for a species?
FYI,  my current species of focus are long ranging (sigma value from suggest.buffer argument is about 3kms.) and it is known the sex of the individual influences their activity ranges.

Regards,

From u118cea91a1619106 at groups.google.com  Sat Jul  8 08:05:12 2023
From: u118cea91a1619106 at groups.google.com (Murray Efford)
Date: Sat, 08 Jul 2023 08:05:12 +0000
Subject: Re: Model fitting
Message-ID: <8ollsXa6qqM.QozvJjxvAwAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <8ollsXa6qqM.nr-nZynHAgAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
References: <8ollsXa6qqM.nr-nZynHAgAJ@secrgroup.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/secrgroup/c/8ollsXa6qqM/m/QozvJjxvAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Obsessing over detection models is unattractive when they do not represent biologically inspired hypotheses (b, bk in your case, unless camera sites were baited) or are likely to have trivial effects on the estimates (t, T in your case). And an exhaustive list of models would be very long. Conversely, you make a case for sex-dependence of sigma and lambda0 (note these go together). So the advice is to think it through. That includes trying a longer-tailed detection function than the default halfnormal (e.g., HEX), because that allows for occasional long movements.
That's my 2 cents worth. Other opinions welcome.
Murray

